# Tribal Warrior

**Alignments.** Any, though selfish alignments are the rarest; typically
principled, scrupulous, unprincipled and aberrant.

The traditional hunter and protector of a Native American tribe - a skilled
wilderness scout, hunter and fighter with no spell-like or supernatural powers,
making up for it in courage, cunning and determination. The book compares him to
the tribe''s grunt, where the other warrior classes are its special forces,
because the spirits choose few people for their greater gifts.

## Fetishes

Tribal Warriors start with two minor fetishes of choice and two major ones: a
Weapon Fetish or the Great Armor Fetish, plus one tattoo fetish. A further minor
fetish is typically awarded at levels 4, 8 and 12, a third major fetish may come
at level 5 for a selfless and courageous warrior, and another for great heroism
no sooner than level 7. A legendary fetish is the dream of a lifetime.

## Lore

Printed 36 notes that, by tradition, only the Tribal Warrior counts as a true
warrior - the other three warrior classes would be thought of as shamans - and
printed 37 that Tribal and Totem Warriors remain mostly male. Native American
warriors fight as hunters do: in small groups, by stealth, ambush and guerrilla
raid, and are often content with an enemy''s surrender or retreat. Most belong to
one of their tribe''s warrior societies. The Simvan, some tribes of wild
Psi-Stalkers, Brodkil, Worm Wraiths and the Xiticix are counted among their
blood enemies.

## Equipment the catalog cannot hold yet

A traditional bow (a modern compound or energy bow for those who do not strictly
follow tradition) with two dozen arrows and six more dipped in silver, a pair of
tomahawks (2D6 S.D.C.), 50 feet of rope, a ball of string, a week of dried meat
and fruit, and war and camouflage paint. A rifle and a revolver - one may be a
Techno-Wizard weapon - may suit a Renegade or some Traditionalists, never a Pure
One.
', 'published', 'data-script'
